{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UNUZxmfbbYeyE61L1V72Cn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# script-analysis — coverage atlas (D1-D20 seen vs. D21+ candidates)\n2\t\n3\tUpdated 2026-10-20 after D20 (1329 ids, 145 members; D20 \"The Butler Did It\" / base `jenkins-pipeline-intrusion` added\n4\tGroovy `.groovy` + a `jenkins_pipeline` host AND a `classic_asp` host for the OLD language VBScript (`.vbs` file holding an ASP\n5\tpage) + 2 Bash; all plain one-line v=3 carriers, no twist; single CORRECT T+13 s 0 wrong, TT 5.0 s, Mastery 11.9 s first try).\n6\tEvery D19-night gap is built except evidence-type-router / file-registry-telemetry-reconcile (given up, 2 attempts each), so\n7\tthe queue below is rebuilt from the D20 direction (CI/build hosts, JVM languages, hosts for prior languages). Read with `reference/techniques/script-analysis.md` (what the GM\n8\tactually asked) — this file is the BREADTH map: which common script/malware-script CTF concepts we have met, which we\n9\thave not, how to fingerprint and statically decode each, and what to prebuild/install before they drop.\n10\t\n11\tContext that drives the likelihood column:\n12\t- Since D8 every new language is a **v=3 polyglot**: 4 fixtures `polyglot-<lang>-{benign-utility,downloader,launcher,\n13\t  staged-chain}` (44 ids: role/attack/adapter/campaign/steps/stage-count/host-profile/obfuscation/stage-profile/\n14\t  next-language + url) plus sometimes one `host-<profile>-<lang>` file. All answers are plan-verbatim; the plan sits\n15\t  behind `HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_V1:<B64|HEX|HB64|FRAG>-..` in the host's comment syntax + a `descriptor_<id>` literal.\n16\t  The carried solver (`challenges/day18/scripting-orphan-process/solver/solve.py`, `V3_MARK` / `_v3_peel` /\n17\t  `analyse_v3`; unchanged D9-D17) is language-agnostic **as long as** the marker data is ASCII/UTF-8 in raw bytes, peelable by\n18\t  hex/base64/reverse within 3 layers. **D18 broke the one-line assumption**: `// HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_V1:SPLIT` carries no data\n19\t  and the plan sits in a multi-line `const HUNTRESS_ANALYSIS_V1_SPLIT_<id> = [\"<TAG>-..\", ..].join(\"\")` array (patched in the\n20\t  D18 solve.py and in lib.codec since D18 night). **D19 broke the raw-bytes assumption**: the ASPX host carries the whole\n21\t  inner script as an escaped string literal (`\\n`, `\\\"`), so marker data runs into `\\n#nullable` and the descriptor is\n22\t  `\\\"`-quoted (lib.codec.v3_plan handles it; the carried solve.py needed a patch). D20 had no carrier twist. The real D21+ risk is not a new language — it is a new **carrier/encoding/container**, a\n23\t  new **synthetic-label family** (D2-D7 era), or a **GM label-regime flip** on an old one (D15 pool-keyed, D16 date-keyed\n24\t  batch `expansion`: same bytes, new label). Narrative prompts (D8 siblings, D15 phantom store, D16 \"prove which are\n25\t  truly equivalent, expose the variant that changed the plot\", D17 \"reconcile source fetch targets with the generated network\n26\t  evidence, find the indicator on only one side\", D18 \"source says which process should spawn which, native telemetry keeps\n27\t  its own record; reconstruct the static process tree, catch the child claiming a parent it never had\") have never been asked\n28\t  -- yet. D17 and D18 each named a SECOND evidence type (network, then process telemetry) that was not shipped; the organizers\n29\t  rotate the evidence type daily, so prepare the pattern (declared-from-source vs observed-in-telemetry, find the one-sided\n30\t  item) for network (rows 68/69, built), process (rows 71/72) and file/registry/schedule telemetry (row 74).\n31\t- Family names may contain `_` (D14 `polyglot-msbuild_xml`); host profiles seen: standalone, wsf_jscript, wsf_mixed,\n32\t  hta_jscript, php_webshell, sct_scriptlet, lua_openresty, perl_cgi, ruby_rack, google_docs_sidebar (D18; D17 no host),\n33\t  aspx_inline_csharp (D19: web-page host, file still named `.csx`), jenkins_pipeline +